from pathlib import Path
import sys
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('14');add=A.add
add('3.2.2-01','Internal energy includes microscopic kinetic and interaction potential energies.','A temperature rise within a phase is associated with an increase in mean particle kinetic energy.','Energy supplied to a sample can increase internal energy, but a temperature rise is not the sole possible outcome. During a phase change, interaction energy can change while temperature stays fixed. A large cooler sample may have more total internal energy than a small warmer sample because the number of particles also matters. Distinguish temperature, internal energy and energy transferred by heating.','Explain how a temperature rise within one phase is related to internal energy.','Mean particle kinetic energy rises.~This contributes to an increase in the body’s internal energy.')
add('3.2.2-02','Thermal capacity describes the energy needed for a specified temperature increase of a whole object.','An object with greater heat capacity has a smaller temperature rise for the same absorbed energy.','The size and material of the body both matter. A large bucket of water needs more energy for a one-degree rise than a small cup of the same water. This is different from specific heat capacity, which is a property per unit mass. The relationship C = mc connects the two for a uniform material over an appropriate temperature interval.','Explain why a larger water sample warms less than a smaller one after equal energy inputs with negligible loss.','The larger sample has a greater heat capacity.~The same energy input is spread over more material, giving a smaller temperature rise.')
add('3.2.2-03','Heat capacity is the energy required to raise a whole object’s temperature by one kelvin.','Its unit is J/K, equivalent to J/°C for a temperature interval.','The quantity refers to the complete object, not one kilogram. It may vary with temperature, so a stated value often applies over a particular interval. When energy is lost to the surroundings, the heater’s total input is greater than the energy absorbed by the object alone.','Define heat capacity.','Heat capacity is the energy required to raise an object’s temperature by one kelvin.')
add('3.2.2-04','Heat capacity is C = Q/ΔT for the absorbed energy and resulting temperature rise.','Rearranging gives Q = CΔT.','A body absorbing 800 J while warming by 4 K has heat capacity 200 J/K. Use the change rather than the final temperature. If heating from 18 to 23 °C, the interval is 5 K, not 296 K. A kelvin offset must not be added to an interval.','Calculate heat capacity when 600 J raises an object’s temperature by 3 K.','C = Q/ΔT = 600/3.~The heat capacity is 200 J/K.')
add('3.2.2-05','Specific heat capacity is the energy required per kilogram for a one-kelvin temperature rise.','Its SI unit is J/(kg K).','This compares materials independently of sample mass under stated conditions. Water has a relatively large value, so a given mass can absorb substantial energy with a modest temperature rise. A material’s specific heat capacity is not the energy for melting it at constant temperature.','Define specific heat capacity.','It is the energy required to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by one kelvin.')
add('3.2.2-06','Specific heat capacity is c = Q/(mΔT).','For heating within one phase, Q = mcΔT with the appropriate approximately constant c.','For 0.50 kg absorbing 2100 J and warming by 10 K, c = 420 J/(kg K). Convert grams to kilograms first. A heater input Pt can estimate Q when the relevant losses and apparatus heating are negligible or corrected. Do not apply this temperature-rise expression to a constant-temperature phase-change interval.','Calculate c for 0.20 kg absorbing 1600 J and warming by 20 K.','c = Q/(mΔT) = 1600/(0.20 × 20).~The specific heat capacity is 400 J/(kg K).')
add('3.2.2-07','A heating experiment can estimate c from measured energy, sample mass and temperature rise.','Energy supplied to the apparatus and surroundings must be reduced or accounted for.','Measure the mass of a solid block, insert a low-voltage heater and thermometer in suitable close-contact holes, and insulate it. Record initial temperature, heater energy from a joulemeter or measured power and time, and final temperature. Calculate c from the energy absorbed by the block. Contact errors, thermal lag and heat loss affect the estimate. For liquids, include stirring and consider the vessel’s heat capacity.','Describe how to estimate a solid block’s specific heat capacity using electrical heating.','Measure its mass, the heater energy and its temperature rise, minimise losses and calculate c = Q/(mΔT).')
add('3.2.2-08','At constant absorbed power and mass, temperature rise is proportional to heating time in the simple no-loss model.','For a fixed absorbed energy and material, temperature rise is inversely proportional to mass.','Combining Q = Pt and Q = mcΔT gives ΔT = Pt/(mc). Test the time relation with one sample and repeated readings, then test mass using different measured masses heated for the same interval with the same power. Keep starting temperature, vessel and surroundings comparable. Longer heating or hotter samples can increase losses, bending the ideal relation.','Describe how to test the inverse relation between mass and temperature rise.','Heat different masses of the same material with equal absorbed energy, measure their rises and compare ΔT against 1/m under controlled conditions.')
add('3.2.2-09','Latent heat is the energy absorbed or released during a phase change without temperature change.','For the whole stated sample it is an energy quantity measured in joules.','The syllabus uses L for total latent heat and l for specific latent heat. Keep the distinction visible because some books use different symbols. A melting sample absorbs energy; freezing releases it under corresponding conditions. A constant temperature does not mean a zero transfer.','Define latent heat for a whole sample.','It is energy absorbed or released during a phase change without a temperature change.')
add('3.2.2-10','Specific latent heat is phase-change energy per kilogram without temperature change.','Its unit is J/kg, and the particular transition must be identified.','Specific latent heat of fusion describes melting or freezing, while vaporisation describes liquid-gas transition. Values differ by substance and conditions. The mass is the amount that changes phase, not automatically the total mass initially in a vessel. Compare the units with specific heat capacity: the latter includes an additional per-kelvin factor.','Define specific latent heat.','It is the energy absorbed or released when 1 kg of a substance changes phase without temperature change.')
add('3.2.2-11','During an ideal constant-temperature phase change, energy changes molecular interaction potential energy.','The mean kinetic energy does not rise while the temperature remains constant.','Boiling separates particles much more widely, requiring energy against attractions. Condensation reverses this and releases energy. Melting changes the ordered structure to a mobile liquid arrangement. Use microscopic interactions rather than saying energy is hidden or absent; latent describes an energy transfer not accompanied by the temperature rise expected within a single phase.','Explain what supplied energy changes during a constant-temperature phase change.','It changes particle arrangement and interaction potential energy.~It does not increase mean kinetic energy during the ideal plateau.')
add('3.2.2-12','Specific latent heat is l = Q/m.','The phase-change energy is Q = ml for the mass that actually changes phase.','If 0.040 kg melts after absorbing 12 000 J at constant temperature, l = 300 000 J/kg. Heating the material to its melting point is a separate energy contribution. A full process may therefore require a temperature-rise stage plus a phase-change stage rather than one formula for both.','Calculate specific latent heat when 0.050 kg changes phase after absorbing 15 000 J at constant temperature.','l = Q/m = 15000/0.050.~The specific latent heat is 300 000 J/kg.')
add('3.2.3-01','Melting and boiling of a pure substance at fixed pressure can proceed at constant temperature.','Continued energy input changes the proportion in each phase.','At the melting plateau, solid and liquid coexist while energy changes the structure. At the boiling plateau, liquid and vapour coexist as energy separates particles. Once the phase change finishes, additional absorbed energy can raise the new phase’s temperature. A heater remaining on is therefore consistent with a horizontal part of a temperature-time graph.','Explain why a heater can remain on while boiling water’s temperature stays nearly constant at fixed pressure.','The input energy supplies the phase change to vapour.~It increases interaction potential energy rather than mean kinetic energy during that interval.')
add('3.2.3-02','Melting point and boiling point identify different phase-change temperatures at a specified pressure.','A pure substance’s characteristic values can support identification when conditions are controlled.','At approximately standard atmospheric pressure, pure water melts near 0 °C and boils near 100 °C. A change in pressure or impurities can change the observed behaviour, so these numbers are not unqualified values for every situation. On a heating curve, distinguish the two plateaux by the phases present and direction of energy transfer.','Explain how to distinguish melting and boiling plateaux on a pure-water heating curve at standard pressure.','Link the lower plateau near 0 °C to solid changing to liquid.~The upper plateau near 100 °C corresponds to liquid changing to vapour.')
add('3.2.3-02-M01','Melting point is the temperature at which a solid changes to liquid at a specified pressure.','At equilibrium the solid and liquid can coexist.','For pure water at standard atmospheric pressure this is near 0 °C. A temperature below that point does not mean a hot liquid is present simply because heating has started; the initial state and supplied energy must be considered.','Define melting point.','It is the temperature at which a solid changes to liquid at a specified pressure.')
add('3.2.3-02-M02','Boiling point is the temperature at which a liquid boils at a specified pressure.','Boiling occurs when its vapour pressure permits bubbles to form throughout the liquid.','At standard atmospheric pressure, pure water’s boiling point is near 100 °C. At lower external pressure it can boil at a lower temperature. Distinguish this from evaporation, which occurs at a surface over a range of temperatures.','Define boiling point.','It is the temperature at which a liquid boils at a specified pressure.')
add('3.2.3-03','Boiling occurs throughout a liquid with vapour bubbles at the boiling condition.','Evaporation occurs at its surface and can occur below boiling temperature.','Faster particles can escape from the surface of a liquid without bubbles forming throughout it. The remaining sample can cool as its mean particle kinetic energy decreases. A puddle can therefore disappear on a day when its temperature is far below 100 °C. Both changes produce vapour, but their locations and temperature conditions differ.','Explain how evaporation differs from boiling.','Evaporation occurs at the surface over a range of temperatures.~Boiling occurs throughout the liquid with vapour bubbles at its boiling condition.')
add('3.2.3-04','A heating or cooling curve plots temperature against time or transferred energy.','Sloped regions represent temperature change within a phase; ideal plateaux represent phase change at fixed pressure.','For an original heating example, plot times 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 min with temperatures −10, 0, 0, 50, 100, 100 °C. Mark melting on the 2–4 min plateau and boiling on the 8–10 min plateau. A cooling example reverses the process: 110, 100, 100, 50, 0, 0 °C at the same times shows condensation then freezing. Plateau durations depend on energy rate and amount, so they are not universal material constants.','Draw the stated water heating curve for times 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 min and temperatures −10, 0, 0, 50, 100, 100 °C, labelling the two phase-change regions.','Put time / min horizontally and temperature / °C vertically, plot the points and join the segments.~Label the horizontal 0 °C region melting and the 100 °C region boiling.')
A.plot('3.2.3-04',[0,2,4,6,8,10],{'Heating':[-10,0,0,50,100,100],'Cooling':[110,100,100,50,0,0]},'Time / min','Temperature / °C','Idealised pure-water curves at standard pressure')
A.misconception('MIS-09','Specific heat capacity and specific latent heat concern different changes.','The first involves temperature rise per kilogram per kelvin; the second involves phase change per kilogram without temperature rise.','A sample warming within one phase uses Q = mcΔT. A sample melting at its fixed melting temperature uses Q = ml. If both occur, separate their energy contributions. The presence of heating alone does not determine which formula is appropriate; identify what happens to the sample.','A substance melts at constant temperature. Explain why Q = mcΔT with ΔT = 0 does not give its required melting energy.','The error is using a temperature-rise equation for a phase change.~The correct relationship is Q = ml for the mass melted, even though ΔT is zero.')
A.misconception('MIS-10','Energy absorbed during a constant-temperature phase change changes interactions, not mean kinetic energy.','A flat temperature trace does not mean zero energy input.','A heater can continuously transfer energy into a boiling sample while particles separate into vapour. The constant temperature indicates approximately unchanged mean kinetic energy. Saying the particles gain average speed throughout the plateau contradicts that temperature condition in this model.','A boiling sample absorbs energy at constant temperature. Explain the error in saying its molecules must have a continuously increasing average kinetic energy.','Constant temperature indicates unchanged mean kinetic energy in this model.~The supplied energy instead increases interaction potential energy as particles separate.')
C=A.choice
C('3.2.2-03','Which unit measures a whole object’s heat capacity?',('J/K','It is energy per temperature rise.'),('J/kg','This is a specific latent-heat unit.'),('J/(kg K)','This is specific heat capacity.'),('W','This is power.'))
C('3.2.2-05','What does specific heat capacity refer to?',('One kilogram warmed by one kelvin','Both mass and temperature interval are included.'),('A whole object melted','That concerns total phase-change energy.'),('One kilogram melted at fixed temperature','That concerns specific latent heat.'),('Energy per second','That is power.'))
C('3.2.2-10','Which unit fits specific latent heat?',('J/kg','It is phase-change energy per mass.'),('J/(kg K)','That includes a temperature-rise factor.'),('K/J','This is an inverse heat-capacity unit.'),('Pa','This measures pressure.'))
C('3.2.3-03','Where does evaporation occur?',('At the liquid surface','Particles escape from the surface.'),('Only in bubbles throughout the liquid','That describes boiling.'),('Only at the boiling point','It can occur below that point.'),('Only inside a solid','A liquid surface is involved.'))
C('3.2.3-01','A pure substance melts at fixed pressure. What can remain constant?',('Temperature','Energy can change arrangement without raising mean kinetic energy.'),('Energy input must be zero','Melting can absorb energy.'),('Solid fraction must be unchanged','It decreases as melting proceeds.'),('Particle positions must stay fixed','The arrangement changes.'))
C('3.2.2-07','Why insulate a heating block?',('To reduce energy loss to surroundings','More heater input then reaches the intended sample.'),('To add mass without measuring it','That would complicate the estimate.'),('To prevent a temperature rise','A rise is needed for this measurement.'),('To remove the need for a thermometer','Temperature change still must be measured.'))
C('3.2.2-01','Which is part of internal energy?',('Microscopic particle kinetic energy','Random motion contributes.'),('The sample’s colour alone','Colour is not an energy store definition.'),('Temperature as a mass','Temperature is not mass.'),('A unit of force','This is not an energy contribution.'))
C('3.2.2-04','What is C when 900 J causes a 3 K rise?',('300 J/K','Divide energy by rise.'),('2700 J/K','This multiplies them.'),('0.00333 J/K','This reverses the ratio.'),('903 J/K','This adds unlike quantities.'))
C('3.2.2-06','For m = 0.5 kg, c = 400 J/(kg K) and ΔT = 5 K, what is Q?',('1000 J','Use mcΔT.'),('4000 J','This divides by mass rather than multiplying.'),('40 J','This uses a different operation.'),('200 J','This omits the temperature rise.'))
C('3.2.2-12','What energy melts 0.10 kg when l = 300 000 J/kg?',('30 000 J','Use ml.'),('3 000 000 J','This divides by mass.'),('300 000 J','This ignores the sample mass.'),('300 J','This misplaces two powers of ten.'))
C('3.2.2-08','With equal absorbed power and time, a doubled mass has what ideal temperature rise?',('Half the rise','ΔT is inversely proportional to mass.'),('Twice the rise','That reverses the mass dependence.'),('Four times the rise','No square relation applies.'),('The same rise','The heat capacity has changed.'))
C('3.2.3-04','On a temperature-time graph, a horizontal phase-change region indicates what?',('Constant temperature during energy transfer','It represents a phase change in the ideal model.'),('No particles remain','Matter remains present.'),('A vertical temperature change','The plotted temperature is constant.'),('Zero elapsed time','The segment extends along time.'))
P=A.part
P('3.2.2-05','State the unit of specific heat capacity.','J/(kg K).',1,'One mark for energy per kg per kelvin.')
P('3.2.2-10','State the unit of specific latent heat.','J/kg.',1,'One mark for joules per kilogram.')
P('3.2.2-11','Explain energy input during constant-temperature melting.','Energy changes the arrangement and interaction potential energy. Mean kinetic energy remains approximately fixed, so temperature does not rise.',2,'One mark for interaction/arrangement change; one for unchanged mean kinetic energy.')
P('3.2.2-06','Calculate energy to warm 0.20 kg by 10 K when c = 400 J/(kg K).','Q = mcΔT = 800 J.',2,'One mark for mcΔT; one for 800 J.')
