A.practical=dict(graph='Plot temperature rise vertically against absorbed energy horizontally for the fixed 0.20 kg sample.',unit='K/J',meaning='The gradient is 1/(mc), reciprocal heat capacity, rather than specific heat capacity itself. For known mass, c = 1/(mass × gradient).',method=['Measure the sample mass and record its initial temperature.','Insulate it and ensure close contact with a low-voltage heater and thermometer.','Supply measured energy in increments using a joulemeter.','Wait for a representative temperature and record the rise after each increment.','Repeat with the same mass and compare the temperature-rise versus energy lines.'],control='Keep the phase unchanged, reduce heat losses, allow for container heating and keep the heater within its safe operating temperature.',table='Record absorbed energy estimate / J, temperature / °C and temperature rise / K.',conclusion='Use the gradient to infer c with the known mass. Curvature may indicate heat loss or changing response, so state the no-loss approximation.')
